.TH CHTTPSVR_STOP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_stop \- close this server's listener
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_stop(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the listener socket that chttpsvr_start registered from the
shared reactor and closes it.  For a Unix domain socket listener it
then removes the socket file, but only while the path still names the
socket that this server created.  The call returns at once, and the
shared reactor and every other registered listener keep running.

Calling it again changes nothing: on a server that never started, or
that has already stopped, chttpsvr_stop does nothing.

It also does nothing, without any error, when srv is CHTTPSVR_NULL
or a stale handle of a server that has already been destroyed.

Connections that the server accepted before this call stay open, and
the server does not close them.  A request in progress runs until it
completes, and a keep-alive connection is served request after
request for as long as the client keeps it open, until the idle
timeout closes it as on a running server.  That is true across a
later restart of the same handle with chttpsvr_start as well, and the
restarted server serves such a connection under its new
configuration.  chttpsvr_destroy waits for these in-flight requests
to drain completely and closes every idle connection that is left
before it frees the resources of the server.

It is safe to call this function with CHTTPSVR_NULL (0).

It may run at the same time as a chttpsvr_start that restarts the
same handle on another thread.  The restart doesn't race this call:
it waits until the old listener is completely torn down here and then
binds a new one.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
This function is synchronous and NOT async-signal-safe.  Do not call
it from a signal handler; call chttpsvr_engine_stop() there.  It
takes internal locks that the interrupted thread may already hold,
for example inside chttpsvr_start() or chttpsvr_destroy(), and the
calling thread then deadlocks on itself.  For a shutdown hook driven
by a signal use chttpsvr_engine_stop(), the only function that is
documented as async-signal-safe, because it moves all of this work
off the interrupted thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_is_running (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
